    ":question/id": "b27b93f8-5f19-566f-95e3-f0a0b21ec684",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-11817",
    ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)={e}^{@@exponent@@},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u=@@exponent@@.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=@@a_value@@.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot @@a_factor@@ \\\\  & =@@ae@@^{u} \\\\  & =@@ae@@^{@@exponent@@}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    ":question/difficulty": {
      ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/easy"
    }
  },
  "q-107420": {
