    ":question/id": "f95fdc58-1eb7-5580-ab34-aae2969c5c74",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-107420",
    ":question/problem": "Calculate ${f}^{'}(x)$ if $f(x)=@@poly@@@@polysign@@{e}^{@@exponent@@}.$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y={e}^{@@exponent@@}$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=@@exponent@@.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}@@=a.@@\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot @@a_factor@@ \\\\  & =@@ae@@^{u} \\\\  & =@@ae@@^{@@exponent@@}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the sum rule of differentiation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(@@poly@@@@polysign@@{e}^{@@exponent@@}) \\\\  & =@@polyprime@@@@polysign@@(@@ae@@^{@@exponent@@}) \\\\  & =@@factored@@^{@@exponent@@}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
