    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-48007",
    ":question/problem": "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=@@ce@@^{@@exponent@@}@@plusd@@.$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=@@ce@@^{u}@@plusd@@,\\,u=@@exponent@@.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=@@ce@@^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x@@}=a.@@\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =@@ce@@^{u}@@dot_a@@ \\\\  & =@@cae@@^{u} \\\\  & =@@cae@@^{@@exponent@@}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
