    ":question/id": "b6722d36-cd26-@@c_e@@84-89df-067b2a93b540",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-48657",
    ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=@@ce@@^{@@exponent@@}$ at the point where $x=@@pointtex@@.$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=@@ce@@^{u},\\,u=@@exponent@@.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=@@ce@@^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x@@}=a.@@\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =@@ce@@^{u}@@dot_a@@ \\\\  & =@@cae@@^{u} \\\\  & =@@cae@@^{@@exponent@@}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=@@pointtex@@$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=@@pointraw@@} & ={(@@cae@@^{@@exponent@@})|}_{x=@@pointraw@@} \\\\  & =@@cae@@^{@@subexp@@} \\\\  & =@@ca_e@@\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x={x}_{1}=@@pointtex@@$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={(@@ce@@^{@@exponent@@})|}_{x=@@pointraw@@} \\\\  & =@@ce@@^{@@subexp@@} \\\\  & =@@c_e@@.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-@@c_e@@ & =@@ca_e@@(x-@@pointtex@@) \\\\ y-@@c_e@@ & =@@caex@@-@@c_e@@ \\\\ y & =@@caex@@\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
