    ":question/problem": "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=@@ce@@^{@@exponent@@}$ at the point where $x=@@point@@.$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=@@ce@@^{u},\\,u=@@exponent@@.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=@@ce@@^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =@@ce@@^{u}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =@@cae@@^{u} \\\\  & =@@cae@@^{@@exponent@@}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=@@point@@$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=@@point@@} & =@@cae@@^{@@subexp@@} \\\\  & =@@cae@@^{@@logk@@} \\\\  & =@@evalproduct@@ \\\\  & @@=slope.@@\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  }
